<%- include('partials/head') %>

<section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
  <div class="hero-copy">
    <h1 id="hero-title">Stops raids with something you can take back.</h1>
    <p class="hero-lede">
      When two separate signals agree, raiders are timed out, and one button reverses the whole
      incident if it got anyone wrong.
    </p>
    <p class="hero-count">In beta, for <strong>Protection Suite</strong> servers.</p>
    <div class="cta">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/pricing">See Protection Suite pricing</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/raidprotector/privacy">What it stores</a>
    </div>
    <p class="hint">
      Report mode is the default. A new install watches and acts on nobody until you say otherwise.
    </p>
  </div>

  <figure class="exhibit">
    <article class="approval incident" data-incident data-mode="report" aria-label="Example incident report">
      <div class="ap-top">
        <span class="badge warn" data-status>
          <svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-warn"></use></svg>
          <span>Possible raid · report only</span>
        </span>
        <span class="example-tag">Example</span>
      </div>
      <p class="ap-title" data-title>Possible raid, incident #0</p>
      <dl class="ap-fields">
        <div class="wide">
          <dt>What fired</dt>
          <dd>
            <ul class="inc-list">
              <li><strong>18</strong> joined in 40s, over a threshold of 6 learned from this server</li>
              <li><strong>14</strong> different accounts posted the same thing, allowing for small edits</li>
            </ul>
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="wide">
          <dt>Who</dt>
          <dd>
            <ul class="inc-list">
              <li><strong>21</strong> accounts looked at</li>
              <li><strong>14</strong> met the two-signal bar</li>
              <li><strong>4</strong> tripped only one signal and were left alone</li>
              <li><strong>3</strong> exempt (owner, established, or out of reach)</li>
            </ul>
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="wide" data-show="report">
          <dt>Not done</dt>
          <dd>
            Report mode. Nothing was done to anyone. This is what would have happened.
            <strong>14</strong> would have been timed out.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="wide" data-show="act">
          <dt>Done</dt>
          <dd>
            <strong>14</strong> timed out. Timeouts expire on their own, and the button below lifts
            every one of them at once.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <p class="ap-foot">Your server</p>
      <div class="stamp stamp-face" hidden aria-hidden="true"></div>
    </article>

    <div class="ap-controls">
      <p class="ap-outcome" role="status"></p>
    </div>

    <p class="act-note">
      In act mode the same report says <strong>14 timed out</strong> and ends with an
      <strong>Undo this incident</strong> button that lifts every one of those timeouts at once.
    </p>

    <figcaption>
      An example report for a made-up raid. RaidProtector posts the real one in your log channel
      when it detects a raid.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<section class="band" aria-labelledby="read-title">
  <div class="band-head">
    <h2 id="read-title">Reading a report</h2>
    <p class="lede">Each part of the report above answers one question.</p>
  </div>
  <dl class="annot">
    <div>
      <dt>What fired</dt>
      <dd>
        <strong>Signals are measured against your server</strong>
        <span>Ten joins a minute is an emergency in one server and a Tuesday in another, so the
        threshold is learned rather than set.</span>
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Who</dt>
      <dd>
        <strong>Two independent signals before anyone is touched</strong>
        <span>A crowd arriving is not a crowd attacking. Joining during a burst is one signal.
        Posting the same text as fifteen others is another. One alone is not enough, because being
        present is not taking part.</span>
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Done</dt>
      <dd>
        <strong>Timeouts, not bans</strong>
        <span>A timeout stops a raid just as dead and expires by itself. Bans are off by default,
        and when they are on they wait behind a delay anyone can cancel.</span>
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Undo this incident</dt>
      <dd>
        <strong>One button undoes all of it</strong>
        <span>RaidProtector records what was there before every action, so the whole incident
        reverses in one press. If part of it cannot be undone, the report names that part.</span>
      </dd>
    </div>
  </dl>
</section>

<section class="band" aria-labelledby="fails-title">
  <div class="band-head">
    <h2 id="fails-title">How it fails</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      The failure it is built to avoid is banning a real member. Every path resolves toward doing
      less.
    </p>
  </div>
  <ul class="ledger">
    <li>
      <span class="badge ok"><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-ok"></use></svg>Safe on its own</span>
      <p class="when">It is not sure</p>
      <p class="what">It does nothing irreversible. Unclear is a real answer, and the timeout that already stopped the raid expires on its own.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="badge ok"><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-ok"></use></svg>Safe on its own</span>
      <p class="when">A long-standing member is caught up in it</p>
      <p class="what">RaidProtector reports anyone who has been here for weeks to you and never punishes them automatically. Raiders can fake an account's age. They cannot fake a month in your server.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="badge ok"><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-ok"></use></svg>Safe on its own</span>
      <p class="when">Your server gets popular overnight</p>
      <p class="what">Two hundred people arriving from a video look identical to a raid in the numbers. It reads what they wrote, so a rush of "hi" does not come out the same as two hundred identical slurs.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="badge warn"><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-warn"></use></svg>Stands down</span>
      <p class="when">It fires repeatedly in a short time</p>
      <p class="what">A circuit breaker stands it down. Several separate incidents in ten minutes means the configuration is wrong, not that the server is under sustained attack.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="badge warn"><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-warn"></use></svg>Stands down</span>
      <p class="when">It has just been added</p>
      <p class="what">It will not act until it can see your member list, because until then a long-standing member looks like a stranger.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="badge crit"><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-crit"></use></svg>Critical</span>
      <p class="when">Its own code has been changed</p>
      <p class="what">It checks itself against a record of what was deployed. If anything differs it stops acting on people everywhere at once and keeps reporting, rather than enforcing rules nobody wrote.</p>
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="band split" aria-labelledby="limits-title">
  <div class="split-lead">
    <h2 id="limits-title">What it cannot do</h2>
    <p class="lede">Stated plainly, so you can decide before adding it.</p>
  </div>
  <dl class="points">
    <div>
      <dt>Work without reading messages</dt>
      <dd>
        Telling a video shout-out apart from a raid cannot be done by counting, so unlike
        BotApprove this one does hold the Message Content intent. Text is reduced to a fingerprint
        in memory and never written to disk. The <a href="/raidprotector/privacy">privacy policy</a>
        says exactly what is kept.
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Catch a patient attacker</dt>
      <dd>
        Detection is thresholds and fingerprints. Someone who stays under yours and varies their
        wording will not trip it.
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Act on the server owner</dt>
      <dd>
        Never, under any evidence. A bot that can depose an owner is a worse weapon than the one
        it defends against.
      </dd>
    </div>
  </dl>
</section>

